Blars Uucp for Osk			March 9, 1992This is distributed as-is, it works for me (on my Qt20x running osk2.3) (the other ports of uucp for osk did not) but may not for you.  Ihave transfered files to and from all unix systems I've tried, and oneMSDOS one.  If I ever get around to putting much more work into UUCPfor Os9, I'll probably start from scratch porting Ion Taylors version. This is a uucp clone for os9/68k.  Since the uucico transfer protocoldoes not have any way to indicate binary or text files, all files arealways transfed in binary mode.  The uucp and uux programs by defaultconvert from osk style line endings (CR) to unix style (LF) onoutgoing files unless a binary option is specified.  Incoming fileswill arive with unix style line endings.  (The trlf program is usefulto convert.)  (One of many bugs I removed from this implementation wasthe inability to transfer binary files.)Blarslib is required.  Trlf and Cron are recomended.Mail is not included with this distribution because I have not yet gota version I consider fully functional.  A fully functional mail packagewill probably be big enough to distribute separatly anyway.I recomend the books "Managing uucp and usenet" and "Using uucp andusenet" for a good overview of what uucp is about and how to configureit.  If your local bookstore doesn't have them you can order themdirectly from O'reilly associates.I've dropped all the documentation that was no longer correct for thisversion, I felt it would be more confusing than useful.You can ask questions of me at blarson@usc.edu.  No guarenteed answer.The following differs from Bob's release of UUCP.Changes by Mike Haaland <Mike@HTSMM1.Las-Vegas.NV.US>The package has been de-blarsed.Setup:/DD/USR/LIB/UUCP needs the following files:Config, Systems, Dialers, Devices and Commands.  A sample Permissions fileis included and partialy implimented.  For examples of these files pleasecheck the CONFIG directory in this archive./DD/SPOOL/UUCP contains spool directories for each system and .Errorand .Log subdirectories.Typical entries in crontab:01 00 * * *         (chx /dd/etc/cmds;uuclean <>>>/nil)30 2,10,18 * * *    (chx /dd/etc/cmds;uupoll jimi <>>>/nil)10,25,40,55 * * * * (chx /dd/etc/cmds;uucico -r1  <>>>/nil)See the file called Changes for more indepth info on the internal changesthat have been made to this package and the utils that have been added.Chat scripts:Chat scripts may contain the following special character sequences tosend certain non-printable and whitespace characters. 	""          expect (or send) nothing 	\d          a two-second delay 	\s          a space 	\r          carriage return 	\n          carriage return 	\m          carriage return 	\l          linefeed 	\B          True line-break 	\U          True line-break 	\T          the phone number given in the Systems file 	\\          a backslash  	\N          a NULL character 	\t          a TAB character 	\b          a BACKSPACE character 	\NNN        ASCII character NNN in octal 	\c          suppress carriage returnA carriage return is automatically sent after each "send" string unlessthe "\c" sequence is used at the end of the string.  All of the abovemay be used in "send" strings.  In an expect string only \s is supported.One other special string may be used, EOT.  If the entire "send" stringconsists of EOT this is the same as using \004\r\004\r\c.Smail-----The TOPS smail is not recommended for this package, but it works.  Betterto use Smail 2.5 and pathalias10 found on the same site you got this packagefrom.  Elm 2.4 PL11, Cnews and RN can also be found there.  Please refer tothe documentaion in those packages for installation, etc.TOPS SMail 2.5--------------To interface this package with the TOPs Smail, place smail in /DD/ETC/CMDSand run pathalias on a local UUCP map for you area.  Smail requires a fewspecial directories./DD/SYS/.LOCKS                 - Lock directory/DD/SYS/.LOCKS/MAIL.LOCKS      - Mail.locks directory for mail files/DD/SYS/.LOCKS/MAIL            - SMail lock directory /DD/SPOOL/MAIL                 - Directory to put local mail'setup' creates a data module that OXM and Smail check to get stringsdefining things like paths to executables and spool directories.  It shouldbe run from your startup script.setup sys/sysinfoHere's and example of a minimal Sysinfo file.  The one I use on 'htsmm1'.# 				SYSINFO##	Hostnamehostname,s=htsmm1##	Host domain hostdomain,s=Las-Vegas.NV.US##	Systemwide pathpath,s=/dd/CMDS:/dd/ETC/CMDS:/dd/USR/CMDS##	Systemwide timezonetimezone,s=PST##	UUCPuux,s=/dd/ETC/CMDS/uux##	Maillmail,s=/dd/ETC/CMDS/lmailsmail,s=/dd/ETC/CMDS/smail## Home- und Spool-Directories##	mailMAIL.SPOOL,s=/dd/SPOOL/MAILMAIL.LOCK,s=/dd/SYS/.LOCKS/MAIL.LOCKSSome files need to be created for smail in the directory /DD/USR/LIB/SMAIL.This directory contains the path data base, and aliases file.  If you onlyforward mail thru ONE host, you may make a map of one entry and feed thatto pathalias.  For example:Say the host you send mail thru is named 'hoptoad', the line reads:smart-host = hoptoadthen run the following script file, map refers to the uucp maps.Either the file you created with the one line entry, or a realuucp map.list map ! pathalias ! lcasep ! qsort >-/DD/USR/LIB/SMAIL/pathsattr  /DD/USR/LIB/SMAIL/paths -rwprnpw-nxdel pathdb.dir pathdb.pag-xmakedb <pathsattr  pathdb.dir pathdb.pag -rwprnpw